WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.535
-Our next guest
is a talented actress

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.337
who made her debut in
the Disney+ series "Ms. Marvel."

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:07.039
She returns as Kamala Khan
in the new movie, "The Marvels,"

00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:09.542
which is in theaters now.
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:10.877
-Whoa!

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:12.345
-Unh!

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:13.779
-Friends of yours?

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:15.782
-Hi.

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:31.164 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:32.632
-Unh!

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:37.437 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:40.773
-Please welcome back to the show
Iman Vellani, everybody.

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:42.542
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:56.522 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:56.522 --> 00:00:58.724
-I am so happy you're back,
Iman.

00:00:58.724 --> 00:01:00.025
-I'm so glad to be here.

00:01:00.025 --> 00:01:01.894
-That is such a fantastic clip.

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:04.430
And I watch a clip like that,
and I think, "Oh, my God.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:07.500
That probably is like
two days of work."

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.769
-I think when
it's really me, one.

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:11.670
-Okay.
-I have a CG double.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.573
-There's a -- oh, CG double.
Okay.

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:14.807
-Yeah, she's great.
-Can you --

00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:16.008
You can't do
the full barrel roll

00:01:16.008 --> 00:01:17.644
in the sky like that?
-Can you?

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:19.679
-No, that's true.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:20.780
How dare you put me
in my place like that?

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.214
-That's two things
we have in common.

00:01:22.214 --> 00:01:24.083
[ Laughter ]
-Before we get to the movie,

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:25.718
I'm so --
I was so excited to hear --

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:27.220
You were watching
"The Sopranos."

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.489
-Yeah.
-And I'm so happy

00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:30.757
when younger people
find that show.

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:32.925
And then it broke my heart
when I realized

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:34.960
it came out
before you were born.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.862
-Uh-oh.
-Yeah.

00:01:36.862 --> 00:01:38.631
I'm not the only one
who's bummed out by that fact.

00:01:38.631 --> 00:01:40.934
-2002, baby.
-2002, baby.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.769
Are you enjoying it?
-I am.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:43.870
Like, every time
I watch a new show,

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.871
I, like, kind of take
that personality.

00:01:45.871 --> 00:01:50.676
I'm like, "Tony and Adriana,
you got all that from gabagool?"

00:01:50.676 --> 00:01:52.611
Like, it's just so good.

00:01:52.611 --> 00:01:54.847
-They must be so happy
they finished filming the movie.

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:56.315
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.315 --> 00:01:58.418
You didn't show up to set and
you're like, "I got a new way

00:01:58.418 --> 00:02:00.619
I'm gonna do the character."
-I know.

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:02.388
No, I've been --
I've been pretty productive.

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:05.258
-Yeah. You -- Another thing
that's very fun for me.

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:08.461
Every time we talk about you
as Ms. Marvel

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:11.030
is that you dressed --
Before you were Ms. Marvel,

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.466
you dressed up like her
on Halloween.

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:14.634
-Yeah.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:15.968
-I mean, that's amazing.
-My grandma helped

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:18.304
sew this whole thing together.
-That's fantastic.

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:20.273
-Now I got a whole department
for that.

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:21.673
-And you still continued to --

00:02:21.673 --> 00:02:25.278
You are a Marvel fan.
You were Tony Stark one year.

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:26.712
[ Laughter ]

00:02:26.712 --> 00:02:29.015
And here you are
as Jubilee.

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:31.351
Is Jubilee recent? Is that?
-That is. Okay.

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:34.320
So, I've, like, been really
into "X-Men" lately.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.921
I am actually writing
a comic book.

00:02:35.921 --> 00:02:37.156
-I know.
We're gonna get to that.

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:38.290
-Okay.
Sorry, I'm spoiling it.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.660
I needed to do a lot
of "X-Men" research,

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:41.828
and I never really read
those comics

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:43.129
because they're so intimidating.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:45.098
There's a million of them.
-Yes, there are, yes.

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:47.900
So I watched, like, the "X-Men"
'90s cartoon in the background.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.169
I had, like, Grant Morrison's
"New X-Men" on my laptop.

00:02:50.169 --> 00:02:52.071
And then I was, like, reading
Jonathan Hickman's new "X-Men."

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:54.340
It was, like, so much "X-Men"
all at once,

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.775
and I just had to dress up.

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:58.177
-Now, when you dress up
in costume,

00:02:58.177 --> 00:03:00.779
I know you've done it as sort
of events where fans are there.

00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:02.148
Do they recognize
you right away,

00:03:02.148 --> 00:03:03.883
or can you sort of
remain anonymous?

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:06.185
-You can, like, see
the gears turning.

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.688
I mean, I was wearing a mask,
which is, like, still nice,

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:10.322
but you stand out more
because people are like,

00:03:10.322 --> 00:03:11.657
"COVID still exists."

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:13.559
-Oh, you're wearing
that kind of mask?

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:15.127
We are talking
about superheroes,

00:03:15.127 --> 00:03:17.664
so forgive me for thinking
this kind of mask.

00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:19.631
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.631 --> 00:03:20.866
So, this is -- this is --

00:03:20.866 --> 00:03:23.536
I'm so -- I'm so impressed
that you did this.

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:26.205
This is your comic
that you wrote, and, uh...

00:03:26.205 --> 00:03:27.573
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:27.573 --> 00:03:29.708
Four issues.
Three are out, one to go.

00:03:29.708 --> 00:03:31.711
-One to go.
-And how have you enjoyed

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:33.345
the process of writing
your own comic book?

00:03:33.345 --> 00:03:35.981
-It's the most
special thing ever.

00:03:35.981 --> 00:03:37.950
I can't be on your show
without bringing up Sana Amanat.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.053
She's the reason this happened.
She co-created the character,

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:43.623
and she made a couple calls
to Marvel editorial

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:45.224
and forced them
to let me write it.

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:48.394
And I really don't know what she
said to them, but it's real.

00:03:48.394 --> 00:03:50.129
And that's my last name,
which is so cool.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:52.097
-Yeah.
-It's great.

00:03:52.097 --> 00:03:53.499
We were just talking about
how cool it is

00:03:53.499 --> 00:03:55.201
to receive art every morning.
-Yes, 'cause you just

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:57.437
write a script,
and then you send it off,

00:03:57.437 --> 00:03:58.938
and then, like,
drawings come back.

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:01.040
You cannot believe it.
-Yeah, it's either the most

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.142
validating thing when they,
like, write --

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:04.776
they draw exactly
what you wrote,

00:04:04.776 --> 00:04:06.479
or you're like, "Okay.

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:09.415
Maybe I need to fix
my writing a little bit."

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:10.916
-Did you have
any hard conversations

00:04:10.916 --> 00:04:12.851
where you realized, not that
they did something wrong,

00:04:12.851 --> 00:04:14.820
but you wrote it
in a way that was unclear

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.321
and they did something right,

00:04:16.321 --> 00:04:18.257
but you got it wrong, and you
had to be like, "I'm so sorry"?

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:19.525
-I'm just gonna say
I'm wrong because they're

00:04:19.525 --> 00:04:21.260
probably going to watch this.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.461
I think it's always the best.

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:24.229
I mean, again, if you --
they're so talented

00:04:24.229 --> 00:04:25.631
that if they draw something
the wrong way --

00:04:25.631 --> 00:04:27.700
That's what we found.
I wrote one with Bill Hader.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.202
-I know.
-Oh, you do?

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:31.904
-I read it, like,
right before coming here.

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:35.107
It is like, you can imagine
two dudes writing that comic.

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:37.876
[ Laughter ]
It's like -- It's like --

00:04:37.876 --> 00:04:39.645
I mean that in the best way
possible.

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:41.681
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:43.149
But, okay --
But listen to the story.

00:04:43.149 --> 00:04:45.885
-It's like -- It's like
Spider-Man, and then --

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:48.387
and then a dude dressed up
as Spider-Man on Halloween,

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:50.422
and he's, like, wasted.
Okay?

00:04:50.422 --> 00:04:52.392
And then the two
of them switch places,

00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:54.026
and the bad guy thinks he got
Spider-Man,

00:04:54.026 --> 00:04:58.130
but he got the drunk Spider-Man.
It's -- It's great.

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:03.068
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:03.068 --> 00:05:05.070
-By the way, I'm --
You might -- I'm --

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:08.440
This is the most anyone's
ever talked about our issue.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.009
This is the best thing
that's ever happened to me.

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:11.076
-I'm glad
I'm putting it on the map.

00:05:11.076 --> 00:05:12.845
-Yeah. And guess what --
joke's on you.

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:15.981
Two dudes did write it.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:15.981 --> 00:05:18.450
Hey, I was surprised to hear
that Billy Joel

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:19.619
was an inspiration
in any way,

00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:21.487
shape, or form
for your comic-book writing.

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.689
-Yes, I am obsessed
with "River of Dreams."

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:26.692
It's like my one of my favorite
albums, my favorite song.

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:28.795
And it's like --
he very much explores,

00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:30.530
you know, where we go in --

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:35.567
I mean, it's a play on stream of
consciousness and dealing with

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:38.537
our inner demons
and, you know, finding yourself.

00:05:38.537 --> 00:05:41.540
And I think that's a lot of what
Kamala's journey is in my book.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:44.744
She's a mutant now, and that's
a whole new label that she

00:05:44.744 --> 00:05:47.245
has to adapt to.
And, you know,

00:05:47.245 --> 00:05:49.615
the entire world's perspective
kind of shifts on her

00:05:49.615 --> 00:05:51.984
now that she has this label
and her own --

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:54.086
how she perceives herself
has shifted.

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:56.989
So, yeah, we literally put her
in her own dreams,

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:59.792
and she has to fight off her
fears and doubts. It's great.

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:01.093
-That's fantastic.
I think you're probably

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:02.795
one of the few people
that uses Billy Joel

00:06:02.795 --> 00:06:04.897
to write your mutant comics.
-Oh, God.

00:06:04.897 --> 00:06:06.131
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.131 --> 00:06:08.267
I hope he serenades me
one day with that song.

00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:10.303
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.303 --> 00:06:12.704
-We got to see, in the clip,
that you got to work

00:06:12.704 --> 00:06:15.507
with the great Sam Jackson.
What is it like?

00:06:15.507 --> 00:06:18.277
Because again, you're not
just a wonderful performer.

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.279
You are also a fan
of these films.

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:22.948
And Sam Jackson, obviously, not
just in the Marvel Universe

00:06:22.948 --> 00:06:25.584
but in the entire cinematic
universe is a big deal.

00:06:25.584 --> 00:06:28.153
What was he like?
-What was he like?

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:29.221
Um...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:29.221 --> 00:06:30.355
Every time I stare at him,

00:06:30.355 --> 00:06:32.324
this like 16x9 frame
would appear around him.

00:06:32.324 --> 00:06:35.094
It was the weirdest thing.

00:06:35.094 --> 00:06:37.096
He's a very generous
scene partner,

00:06:37.096 --> 00:06:38.497
a very encouraging
scene partner.

00:06:38.497 --> 00:06:41.233
I was doing that fight scene
with him, actually,

00:06:41.233 --> 00:06:44.937
and he -- I wanted
to try something new,

00:06:44.937 --> 00:06:48.006
so I high-fived him
at the end of the scene.

00:06:48.006 --> 00:06:50.743
-Right.
-Creative choices, people.

00:06:50.743 --> 00:06:52.812
And then -- And then we cut,
and Sam goes,

00:06:52.812 --> 00:06:54.413
"They won't use that take."

00:06:54.413 --> 00:06:56.048
[ Laughter ]

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:58.250
"Do you know why
they won't use that take?"

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:01.187
And I was like...

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:03.556
And he's like, "Your hand
was covering your face."

00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:05.824
-Ahh!
-And I was like, "Oh, my God.

00:07:05.824 --> 00:07:08.661
I'm so sorry
I ruined this for us!"

00:07:08.661 --> 00:07:10.529
And he's like, "Okay.
On the next one, elbow me."

00:07:10.529 --> 00:07:12.631
And I was like, "What?"
He's like, "Yeah, elbow me."

00:07:12.631 --> 00:07:14.133
I was like, "Okay."
So we get to the end

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:15.968
of the scene, and I, like,

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:17.636
so scared of touching
Sam Jackson.

00:07:17.636 --> 00:07:20.206
I like lightly elbowed him,
and then we cut.

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:24.276
He's like, "No, no no no!
Elbow me!"

00:07:24.276 --> 00:07:25.945
And I was like,
"Okay, I'm sorry!"

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:27.980
We do another take.
I fully chickened out.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:30.649
Could not figure out
the maneuvering of an elbow.

00:07:30.649 --> 00:07:32.351
Did not do it, and he goes --

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:36.221
He grabs my arm and, like,
turns to me, and he's like,

00:07:36.221 --> 00:07:39.892
"Elbow me, mother [bleep]!"
And I was like, "Listen..."

00:07:39.892 --> 00:07:43.796
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:07:43.796 --> 00:07:46.399
For the record,
he didn't actually swear at me.

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:49.000
That's just how I heard it.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.737
I was trying to get him
to swear at me for so long.

00:07:51.737 --> 00:07:53.405
-He wouldn't do it.
-Nothing but hugs.

00:07:53.405 --> 00:07:55.341
-Yeah, nothing but hugs.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:07:55.341 --> 00:07:57.176
I'm so glad you gave us

00:07:57.176 --> 00:07:59.178
the multiverse version
of Sam Jackson.

00:07:59.178 --> 00:08:00.613
[ Laughter ]

00:08:00.613 --> 00:08:03.348
You told the story where he did
call you a mother [bleep].

00:08:03.348 --> 00:08:05.685
-Yeah, it was a dream.
-It was a dream.

00:08:05.685 --> 00:08:06.818
I love that those
are your two dreams --

00:08:06.818 --> 00:08:09.288
"Oh, my God. If Billy Joel
would serenade me

00:08:09.288 --> 00:08:11.389
while Sam Jackson
called me a mother [bleep]."

00:08:11.389 --> 00:08:14.126
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:08:14.126 --> 00:08:16.462
"What more could a kid
ever ask for?"

00:08:16.462 --> 00:08:17.830
-I want that on my gravestone.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.231
-Yes, exactly.

00:08:19.231 --> 00:08:23.336
Hey, you know, because you are
informed on this universe,

00:08:23.336 --> 00:08:24.636
I heard there's times on set

00:08:24.636 --> 00:08:26.872
where you actually
catch something

00:08:26.872 --> 00:08:29.041
that maybe doesn't
make perfect sense.

00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:31.777
-Yes. I've e-mailed
our director, Nia DaCosta,

00:08:31.777 --> 00:08:33.813
like, a laundry list
of plot holes,

00:08:33.813 --> 00:08:35.514
and, like,
"I don't think Sam Jackson

00:08:35.514 --> 00:08:37.783
would actually say this,

00:08:37.783 --> 00:08:39.352
but here's what
he could say instead."

00:08:39.352 --> 00:08:40.953
I'm sure
she very much appreciated

00:08:40.953 --> 00:08:43.022
all of that unsolicited advice.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:43.022 --> 00:08:45.090
But, no, there was, like,
literally one day on set,

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:47.559
I pointed something out really
early while we were filming it,

00:08:47.559 --> 00:08:48.860
and I'm like,
"This doesn't make sense."

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:50.162
And they were like,
"Iman, just like, chill.

00:08:50.162 --> 00:08:53.032
Sit down for one second."

00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:55.368
And then, like,
half the day goes by,

00:08:55.368 --> 00:08:57.102
and then suddenly
Nia notices.

00:08:57.102 --> 00:08:58.804
"Hey, this actually
doesn't make sense."

00:08:58.804 --> 00:09:00.772
And then Teyonah goes,
"No, you're right."

00:09:00.772 --> 00:09:02.975
And I'm sitting there.

00:09:02.975 --> 00:09:04.777
"It's almost like someone
told you."

00:09:04.777 --> 00:09:05.778
[ Laughter ]

00:09:05.778 --> 00:09:07.679
Um, so, yeah, I only --

00:09:07.679 --> 00:09:09.915
-So you were cool about.
-I was cool about it, totally.

00:09:09.915 --> 00:09:11.183
It's not in the movie
anymore, either, so...

00:09:11.183 --> 00:09:13.185
-All right.
Well, you were right again.

00:09:13.185 --> 00:09:15.988
And it is just so wonderful
to have you back here.

00:09:15.988 --> 00:09:18.124
I am so thrilled that
the strike is over

00:09:18.124 --> 00:09:21.026
so you can be out promoting
this wonderful film

00:09:21.026 --> 00:09:24.463
and your wonderful comic.
You guys, that's Iman Vellani.

00:09:24.463 --> 00:09:26.565
"The Marvels"
is in theaters now.

00:09:26.565 --> 00:09:27.966
Her comic book is on the racks.

00:09:27.966 --> 00:09:30.202
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

